---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import { Card, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Macro Strategy - Replenish Guide"
    description="Master the foundational macro-concepts of competitive Premodern Replenish. Learn to navigate dual-angle disruption, identify archetype roles, and maintain rigorous selection discipline."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="Macro Strategy"
        description="Master the foundational macro-concepts that dictate competitive game flow—navigating bi-directional disruption vectors, executing patient endpoint control, and maintaining strict resource discipline."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/swords.jpg"
        imageAlt="Swords to Plowshares"
        imageCaption="Swords to Plowshares by Kaja Foglio"
        isCard={true}
    />

    <Card title="The Two-Angle Disruption Framework" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            Parallax Replenish excels at insulating its game plan against
            single-axis strategies. Pure aggression, linear mana denial, or slow
            countermagic suites can all be systematically dismantled when
            confronted in isolation. True strategic tension emerges when an
            opposing archetype attacks across two intersecting angles
            simultaneously—such as an early aggressive clock backed by severe
            land destruction (<a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Wasteland">Wasteland</a
            >, <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Rishadan Port"
                >Rishadan Port</a
            >) or fast combat pressure supported by cheap permission.
        </p>
        <div
            style="background-color: var(--w-color-primary-95); padding: 1rem; border-radius: var(--w-md-radius); border-left: 3px solid var(--w-color-primary-40); margin-top: 1.25rem;"
        >
            <strong
                style="color: var(--w-color-primary-10); display: block; margin-bottom: 0.5rem;"
                >Mulligan Discipline Under Dual-Angle Pressure</strong
            >
            When confronting dual-angle pressure, attempting to sculpt out of a misaligned
            starting hand across turns one and two is mathematically fatal because
            dedicated card selection engines begin at three mana. For quantitative
            heuristics and hand evaluation frameworks under disruption, consult our
            dedicated <a
                href="/mulligan"
                class="guide-link"
                >Mulligan Guide</a
            >.
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Role Identification & Pace of Play" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            In competitive Premodern, Parallax Replenish is a dynamic
            Control-Combo archetype with multi-angled post-sideboard
            capabilities. Succeeding across a full match requires accurately
            identifying your strategic role based on your opponent's clock and
            interaction suite:
        </p>
        <div
            style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;"
        >
            <div>
                <h3
                    style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10); font-size: 1.1rem;"
                >
                    Disruptive Control & Fast Combo Pivot
                </h3>
                <p style="margin: 0;">
                    Against fast aggressive clocks and burn structures (such as
                    Goblins, Sligh, or UG Madness), your goal is not passive
                    long-term survival—you must actively disrupt their momentum
                    and control the battlefield just long enough to assemble
                    your combo as rapidly as possible. Deploy early defensive
                    interaction like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Wave">Parallax Wave</a
                    >, spot removal, and table resets to stall their clock,
                    while aggressively sculpting your graveyard to force a
                    decisive lethal untap before their direct damage reach or
                    explosive topdecks close the gap.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3
                    style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10); font-size: 1.1rem;"
                >
                    The Proactive Combo Role
                </h3>
                <p style="margin: 0;">
                    Against traditional inevitability control decks or faster
                    pure combo setups, you must assume an urgent proactive
                    stance. Your imperative shifts to aggressively sculpting
                    your graveyard in early turns and forcing through an
                    operational <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
                    > or hardcasting <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > before their defensive countermagic perimeter fully solidifies.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3
                    style="margin: 0 0 0.5rem 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-10); font-size: 1.1rem;"
                >
                    The Transformational Midrange Role
                </h3>
                <p style="margin: 0;">
                    In post-sideboard games, when opponents severely dilute
                    their primary game plans with graveyard hate like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
                    > or <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Planar Void">Planar Void</a
                    >, Replenish can transform into a potent white-blue midrange
                    deck. By deploying proactive disruptive armor like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Defense Grid">Defense Grid</a
                    > alongside efficient threat engines such as <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Meddling Mage">Meddling Mage</a
                    >, you completely circumvent their reactive hate
                    suite—closing out the game through superior battlefield
                    presence and evasive combat clocks while they sit on dead
                    graveyard interaction.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Endpoint Play & Resource Preservation" titleTag="h2">
        <p>
            Against heavy permission decks, evaluate game state progression
            through the lens of terminal endpoints. Imagine a hypothetical final
            state where both players draw out their entire libraries: if your
            ultimate win-condition density overcomes their absolute countermagic
            count, you are statistically favored to win prolonged attrition
            battles. In such matchups, total resource preservation is
            paramount—avoid executing speculative cantrips or discarding
            high-value cards for trivial temporary advantages.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
            In protracted counter-wars, if a control opponent retains eight
            permission spells while you assemble four copies of <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
            >, four <a href="#" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Opalescence"
                >Opalescence</a
            >, and three <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
            >, your terminal spell will ultimately resolve—provided you have not
            frittered away redundant engine pieces in early exchanges.
        </p>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Attunement Resource Discipline" titleTag="h2">
        <Alert theme="warning">
            <strong>Never Activate Attunement Blindly</strong><br />
            Before resolving an activation, you must confidently answer two analytical
            criteria: <em>which exact cards in your hand are expendable</em>,
            and <em
                >what precise mechanism or answer is required from the top of
                your deck?</em
            > If either answer remains ambiguous, strictly forego the activation.
        </Alert>
        <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 0;">
            A prevalent execution error among developing pilots is activating <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Attunement">Attunement</a
            > repeatedly without a definitive resource plan. Particularly against
            interactive control structures, every reckless filtering loop risks stripping
            irreplaceable lands or protective win-condition redundancy—exposing your
            late-game posture to fatal single-point answers. Always maintain documented
            purpose for every discarded card, and cast <a
                href="#"
                class="card-hover"
                data-card-name="Frantic Search">Frantic Search</a
            > strictly during your opponent's end step to maximize operational untap
            safety.
        </p>
    </Card>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
